      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Limiting the liability with a cap sounds like a smart move. \n\nOne more thing though—the phrase \"indemnify, defend, and hold harmless\" seems like a lot of different things. Is there a practical difference between \"defending\" them and just \"indemnifying\" them? Like, does \"defending\" mean I have to actually pay for their lawyers while the case is still going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The three words \"indemnify, defend, and hold harmless\" are often used together, but they're not exactly interchangeable. Think of it like a three-part shield that the Disclosing Party is trying to protect themselves with.\n\n\"Indemnify\" means that you'll cover the Disclosing Party's losses or damages, as we discussed earlier. It's like providing a financial safety net to protect them from financial losses.\n\n\"Defend\" means that you'll take action to protect the Disclosing Party's interests in a lawsuit or other legal proceeding. This can include hiring your own lawyers, filing counterclaims, or taking other steps to defend the Disclosing Party's position. However, it doesn't necessarily mean that you'll pay for the Disclosing Party's lawyers while the case is still going on.\n\n\"Hold harmless\" is a bit more nuanced. It means that you'll not only cover the Disclosing Party's losses or damages, but also take responsibility for any actions or omissions that might lead to those losses or damages. It's like saying, \"I'm not just covering your losses, I'm also taking responsibility for my own actions that might have caused those losses.\"\n\nIn practice, \"defending\" the Disclosing",
      "source": "target",
